artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider a class N of synchronous finite-state neuronal networks defined as follows. A network in N consists of n nodes (neurons), each carrying a state in a finite set S (for example, S = {0, 1, ..., R} where R encodes a refractory period, with state 0 representing rest and state 1 representing firing). The network is endowed with a directed graph D on the n nodes whose edges carry signed weights (excitatory or inhibitory). At each discrete time step, all neurons update their states synchronously according to a fixed deterministic rule that depends on the weighted sum of the current states of the in-neighbors of each neuron and a firing threshold; after firing, a neuron progresses through the refractory states before returning to rest.

For a network in N drawn at random by sampling its underlying directed connectivity D from a prescribed random digraph model (with edge weights chosen by an independent fixed mechanism), study the typical asymptotic and transient dynamics of the resulting deterministic discrete-time dynamical system on S^n. In particular, characterize:
  (a) the attractor structure (number, sizes, and periods of attracting cycles, basin sizes);
  (b) transient behavior (distribution of transient lengths before reaching an attractor);
  (c) phase transitions in the above quantities as parameters of the random connectivity model vary.

Carry out this analysis for each of the following random digraph models in place of the (single-type) Erdős–Rényi digraph G(n, p):
  1. multitype Erdős–Rényi random digraphs, where the n nodes are partitioned into finitely many types and the probability of a directed edge from a node of type i to a node of type j is a prescribed parameter p_{ij};
  2. generic scale-free random digraphs, in which the in- and/or out-degree distributions follow a power law;
  3. small-world random digraphs (for instance, of Watts–Strogatz type), obtained by random rewiring of a regular directed graph.

Determine, for each model, the typical attractor behavior, transient behavior, and phase transitions of the network dynamics in the large-n limit.
